# Install React with TypeScript (beginner)

React is not an app you download like Chrome. You **create a project folder**; that project includes React and TypeScript.

You need **Node.js** first (that gives you `npm`). In Terminal, run `node -v`. If that fails, follow `docs/tutorials/REACT/node.js_install.md`, then come back here.

This uses **Vite**, the usual way to start a basic React app.

---

## 1. Open Terminal

Mac: `Command + Space`, type `Terminal`, press Return.

Go to the folder where you keep projects:

```bash
cd ~/Documents
```

---

## 2. Start the installer

```bash
npm create vite@latest
```

The first time, npm may ask to download `create-vite`. That is expected.

### How to answer the questions

- **Type** your answer, then press **Return**.
- On a **list**, use the **up/down arrow keys** to move the `❯` marker, then press **Return**.
- Do not pick options that end with `↗` (those open extra tools, not a basic app).

---

## 3. Answer each prompt (TypeScript React)

The wording can vary slightly by version. Use this table.

| Prompt | What to do |
| --- | --- |
| `Ok to proceed? (y)` | Type `y` and press Return |
| `Project name:` | Type `my-react-app` and press Return (or press Return to keep the default name) |
| `Package name:` | Press Return to keep the suggestion (this one often does not appear) |
| `Select a framework:` | Arrow to **React**, then Return. Skip Vanilla, Vue, and the rest. |
| `Select a variant:` | Arrow to **TypeScript**, then Return. That is the basic TypeScript app. |
| `Install with npm and start now?` | Type `y` and press Return if you want the easy path (install + open the app). Type `n` if you want to run the commands yourself (section 4). |
| Target directory is not empty | Choose **Cancel operation**. Pick a new project name instead of deleting files. |

### Which “variant” is the basic one?

Under React you may see several rows. For a first app, choose **exactly**:

**TypeScript**

Do **not** choose:

- **JavaScript** — that is not TypeScript
- **TypeScript + React Compiler** — extra, not needed yet
- **RSC**, **React Router**, **TanStack Router**, or anything with **↗** — those are other starters

---

## 4. If you answered “No” to install and start

The folder is created, but packages are not installed yet:

```bash
cd my-react-app
npm install
npm run dev
```

Use the folder name you typed at `Project name:` if it was not `my-react-app`.

`npm install` can take a minute. That is normal.

---

## 5. Open the app

If the installer already started the app, Terminal will print a link.

If you ran `npm run dev` yourself, you get the same link, usually:

`http://localhost:5173`

Hold `Command` and click it (or paste it into a browser). You should see the default Vite + React page.

Leave that command running while you work. Stop it with `Ctrl + C`. Start it again with `npm run dev` from inside the project folder.

---

## 6. You are done

React + TypeScript live **inside** your project folder. Open that folder in your editor.

| File | What it is |
| --- | --- |
| `src/App.tsx` | Start editing here |
| `src/main.tsx` | Starts React and attaches it to the page |
| `tsconfig.json` | TypeScript settings (you can ignore this at first) |

`.tsx` means a TypeScript file that can contain React markup (JSX). That is normal for this setup.

To make another app later, `cd ~/Documents` again and run `npm create vite@latest` with a new project name.

---

## If something fails

| What you see | What to do |
| --- | --- |
| `command not found: npm` | Install Node, then **quit and reopen** Terminal |
| Stuck on a list | Use **arrow keys**, then **Return** — clicking with the mouse often does nothing |
| Picked the wrong variant | Delete the folder (only if it is the new empty project) and run `npm create vite@latest` again |
| Browser is blank / won’t load | Make sure `npm run dev` is still running and use the URL Terminal printed |
| Port already in use | `Ctrl + C`, then `npm run dev` again — Vite may use `5174` |

---

## Shortcut next time (optional)

When you already know the answers, this creates the same **React + TypeScript** app without the menus:

```bash
npm create vite@latest my-react-app -- --template react-ts --no-interactive
cd my-react-app
npm install
npm run dev
```
